<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qu Yuan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 340–278 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qu Yuan (c. 340–278 BCE) was a poet and minister of the State of Chu, traditionally regarded as the first great individual poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Exiled after his advice to King Huai was rejected, he is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-sao">Li Sao</a> and other poems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-ci">Songs of Chu</a>. According to tradition he drowned himself in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miluo-river">Miluo River</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> capture of the Chu capital, an act commemorated by the Dragon Boat Festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qu-yuan.jpg" alt="Qu Yuan" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chen Hongshou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qu_Yuan_Chen_Hongshou_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
